    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leak, they change. We'll get to what this means \nfor black holes and for the universe in another  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686640,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "episode of Spacetime. In the very first instant \nafter the Big Bang, the density of matter was so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693680,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "great everywhere that vast numbers of black holes \nmay have formed. These primordial black holes may  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still be with us. There's no longer any question \nthat black holes exist. LIGO's recent observation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707760,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gravitational waves for emerging black holes is \na stunning confirmation of this fact. Of course,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we already thought they must exist as long as a \nvolume of space contains a high enough density of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719760,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass or energy. General relativity tells us that \na black hole will form. In the modern universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there's only one natural way to get such insane \ndensities. That's in the core of the most massive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732720,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars when they die. The process is awesome and \nwe look at it in a previous video, but that's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739440,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the modern universe. Once upon a time, the entire \nuniverse had the density of a stellar corpse. In  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fact, soon after the big bang, the density of the \nuniverse was vastly higher. So why didn't all the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter in the universe become black holes then? \nWell, actually, some of it may have formed what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758960,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we call primordial black holes, and they may \nstill be around today. Let's back up a bit. In  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765680,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "order to make a black hole, extremely high density \nisn't enough. You need a density differential.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Otherwise, there's no preferred direction \nfor all that gravitational attraction. Also,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gravitational pull needs to be strong enough \nto overcome the expansion of the universe. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter in the early universe was pretty smoothly \nspread out and the universe was expanding fast.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means most of it avoided collapsing into \nblack holes. And that's a very good thing, by the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797040,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way. However, it wasn't perfectly smooth. There \nwere lumps. The oldest light we can see is the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic microwave background radiation. It reveals \ntiny differences in the density of matter from one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810240,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point in space to the next. The universe was very \nslightly lumpy at the moment the CMBB was created  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about 400,000 years after the Big Bang. These \ndensity fluctuations were enough to kickstart the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823200,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formation of galaxies, but certainly not enough \nto immediately collapse into black holes. Yet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we rewind time, those fluctuations must have \nbeen much stronger. It's thought that these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluctuations originally formed when the entire \nobservable universe was smaller than a single  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842800,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atom. Back then, quantum fluctuations caused a \nsort of static fuzz across the minuscule cosmos.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851680,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are several different stories for the \ninitial size and growth of these fluctuations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857760,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and cosmic inflation certainly plays a role. But \nit's well within the possibility of many models  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864240,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that some of these fluctuations were at some point \nin the early expansion intense enough to resist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871760,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the local expansion of the universe and form black \nholes. Some highly speculative big bang physics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880000,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also predicts primordial black holes. For example, \nthe collapse of cosmic string loops and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886800,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collision of bubble universes. Awesome. Now, these \nmodels can predict a huge range of possible masses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894400,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for primordial black holes. PBH's as we like to \ncall them in the biz. PBHs could have been formed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at a few grams to tens of thousands of times the \nmass of the sun depending on which formation model  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you go with, or they might not exist at all. \nThat's a big possibility. If they do exist,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then there's probably a particular mass range \nthat most of them formed at. Discovering PBH's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919360,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and learning their masses would tell us a huge \namount about the earliest moments of our universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We need to hunt for these black holes or their \ninfluence in the modern universe. First of all,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we aren't going to find primordial black \nholes less than around a billion tons or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mass of a small asteroid. They would have \nall evaporated away due to Hawking radiation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'll get back to that. Black holes larger than \nthis should still be around, but they'd be very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949840,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difficult to spot being so black and all. If PBHs \nare rare, then it may be impossible to confirm or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disprove their existence entirely. However, \nthere is a question that we can answer with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962400,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some certainty. Could primordial black holes \nbe dark matter? This is a slightly terrifying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971200,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possibility that 80% of the mass in the universe \nis in the form of countless swarming black holes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979680,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's a lot of primordial black holes. And so we \nexpect them to leave their mark on the universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986720,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in different ways. For one thing, if these little \nknots of warped spaceime are everywhere, then they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should produce obvious gravitational lensing. \nWe'd expect them to frequently pass in front of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000080,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other space stuff. Depending on PBH mass, this \nwould cause a twinkling effect. microl lensing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in stars in our galaxy, in distant quazars, even \nin gammaray bursts. And well, we just don't see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014720,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough of this twinkling, which rules out a lot of \npossible masses. There's also the fact that swarms  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021840,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of black holes would mess up their surroundings. \nAs the heavier ones buzz around the galaxy, they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should pull apart loosely bound binary systems and \nhave an effect on the structure of star clusters.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1034400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The smallest should fall into neutron stars, \ncausing them to either explode or become black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040080,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes themselves, but we see loosely bound \nbinaries and normal star clusters and plenty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of neutron stars. These arguments let us rule \nout all but a very narrow set of mass ranges for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053520,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "primordial black holes as an explanation for dark \nmatter. The options we're left with are either  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060960,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lots of PBH's with masses similar to a large \nasteroid like series, so around 10 ^ of 21 kg,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069360,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or a much smaller number of really big PBHs, \naround 20 to 100 times the sun's mass. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077120,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this last possibility is sketchy. Some scientists \nthink that the voracious feeding of lots of really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083360,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big primordial black holes would have left their \nmark on the cosmic microwave background. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "others argue that the recent LIGO detection \nof the merging of two approximately 30 solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1096000,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass black holes is evidence in favor of this \nidea. With new observations from both regular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "telescopes and LIGO, we're rapidly closing \nall of these mass windows. Before too long,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109360,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we'll either spot the signature of primordial \nblack holes at these masses or discover that PBH's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1116160,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are actually very rare and that they're certainly \nnot dark matter. This latter is more likely, but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1123840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we'll see. Of course, primordial black holes that \nhave already evaporated due to Hawking radiation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1129840,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "definitely are not dark matter, and that rules \nout any PBH is lighter than about a billion tons.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1136640,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that last stage of Hawking evaporation is very \nfast. In fact, it's explosive. It's possible that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1145200,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "certain types of very short gammaray bursts are \nthese final flashes from PBH's evaporating in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1152000,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our galaxy. Some highly speculative stuff, \nbut also some highly awesome possibilities.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It wouldn't be right to end a discussion on \nprimordial black holes without talking about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1164400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what would happen if one passed through the \nsolar system. Even a close encounter with a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1170000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole as massive as the sun or higher would \nbe pretty catastrophic. If it passed anywhere near  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1176080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the planetary system, the gravitational \ntug would disrupt the planet's orbits.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1181760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even if it passed by the outskirts of the solar \nsystem, it could shake up the orc cloud and send  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1186560,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a nice rain of comets to pepper the inner solar \nsystem. Of course, regular black holes from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1193040,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supernova can and perhaps have done that. Having \nhighmass primordial black holes just makes it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1200480,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more likely. If PBH's are closer to the mass of a \nlarge asteroid, then they're too low in mass and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1207440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably moving too fast to do any gravitational \ndamage. They'd just zip right through the solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1213120,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system unnoticed. It's a different matter if one \nhit the Earth traveling at a couple hundred km/s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1220560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it would punch straight through the planet, but \ncertainly leave a narrow column of vaporized  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1225520,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rock behind it. These sorts of hits would be \nincredibly rare and may never happen. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1232800,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if primordial black holes have approximately \nthe minimum possible mass to not have evaporated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1239520,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around a billion tons, these would be much more \nabundant than asteroid mass PBH's. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1246720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they may pass through the planet frequently. A \nbillion ton black hole has an event horizon around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1252240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the size of a proton. So, it would pass through \nthe planet as though the Earth were made of air.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1258880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, it would deposit something like a \nbillion jewels of Hawking radiation on its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1263760,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way through. This should leave detectable traces \nin crystalline material in Earth's crust. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1271280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps geologists will be the first to discover \nprimordial black holes. If they're out there,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1277200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "someone will figure it out. I mean, how long \ncan the universe expect to hide vast numbers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1282240,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of holes punched in the fabric of spaceime? The \nsingularity, the point of infinite density at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1290240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "core of a black hole, but also so much more. \nIn mathematics, singularities come in wild  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1296320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and wonderful varieties. The black hole itself \ncontains more than one. Isaac Newton's universal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1302320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "law of gravitation was an incredible insight when \nhe figured it out in the late 1600s. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1308320,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we still use it to fly spacecraft around the solar \nsystem today. However, it has its problems. Let's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1315760,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look at the math. Newton's equation gives you the \ngravitational force exerted between two masses m1  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1323200,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and m2 that are distance r apart. Straightforward \nenough, that r squared in the denominator spells  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1331360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trouble. It means the force gets larger the \ncloser the masses are to each other. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1336800,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "makes sense. But what about when r gets really \nclose to zero? Then the result of the equation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1343680,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the force becomes extremely large and is infinite \nwhen r becomes equal to zero. That doesn't really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1352080,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make a lot of sense. Infinite force means infinite \nacceleration, which means well, physics breaks.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1359760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to Newton's law, in order to feel \nthat infinite gravitational acceleration,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1364800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you need to get zero distance from an object's \ncenter of mass. That means all of that object's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1370080,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass would need to be concentrated at that center, \na single point of zero size, which means infinite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1377440,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "density, and that of course would make it a \nblack hole. We often use the word singularity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1383840,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to describe the hypothetically infinitely \ndense core of a black hole. But in math,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1390400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the meaning of this word is much more general. \nYou know what? Instead of me trying to explain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1396240,
