    "tStartMs": 2106960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both moving and stationary observers to agree \non how much older everyone looks at the end of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2113040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the journey. Everyone agrees on the number of \nticks that occurred on everyone else's clock.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2119120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They just don't agree on the duration of all of \nthose ticks. Reminder, time measured by a moving  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2125600,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observer on their own clock is called proper time. \nBut counting those clock ticks isn't the best way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2131920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for everyone to agree on space-time relationships. \nThere's this thing called the space-time interval  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2138320,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that relates observer dependent perspectives \non the length and duration of any journey that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2145440,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all observers will agree on. even if they don't \nagree on the delta x and deltat t of that journey.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2152560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've talked about it before, but it's a tricky \nconcept to understand intuitively. But we want  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2158240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that intuition because more than proper time, the \nspace-time interval defines the flow of causality.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2164960,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In relativity, 3D space and 1D time become a \nsingle 4D entity called spacetime. To preserve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2172320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our sanity, we represent this on a space-time \ndiagram, plotting time and only one dimension of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2178720,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space. We'll see our causal geometry emerge plain \nas day. Even in this simplified picture, there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2185680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is no standing still on a space-time diagram. \nIf I don't move through space, I still travel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2191200,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forward in time at a speed of exactly 1 second \nper second. According to my proper time clock,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2198400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "motion at a constant velocity appears as a sloped \nline and the time axis is scaled so that the speed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2204720,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of light is a 45° line. Now, let's say we have a \ngroup of space-time travelers. They start at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2212480,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "origin where x and t equal zero. They race away to \nthe left and the right for 5 seconds according to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2219440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their own watches. They all travel at different \nspeeds, some close to the speed of light, but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2224880,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never faster. The path they cut through spaceime \nis called their world line. My world line is only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2232320,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through time. And the tick marks on the time axis \ncorrespond to my own proper time clock ticks. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2239200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "faster a traveler moves, the longer their world \nline. That's not just because of their speed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2244800,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "though. To me, their clocks tick slow. They \ntime their journey on these slow clocks. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2251120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I perceive them traveling for longer. Accounting \nfor this, we find that our space-time travelers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2256800,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are arranged on a curve that looks like this. This \nshape is a hyperola. Drawing a connecting line at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2264400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the tick of every traveler's proper time clock \ngives us a set of nested hyperbole. But these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2270320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aren't just a pretty pattern. These curves are \nkind of the contours defining the gradient of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2275680,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causality down which time flows and etched into \nspaceime by the equations of special relativity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2282800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to understand why, we need to see how \nthese proper time contours appear to other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2287760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space-time travelers. Instead of doing that with \nequations, we can see it with geometry. First,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2294720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need to draw the space-time diagram from \nthe perspective of one of the other travelers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2300480,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To transform the diagram, we need to figure out \nwhat they see as their space and time axes. Time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2307440,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is easy. They see themselves as stationary. \nSo, their time axis is just their own constant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2314320,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "velocity world line. and their x-axis. Well, from \nmy stationary point of view, I define my x-axis as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2322240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a long string of space-time events at different \ndistances, but that all occur simultaneously at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2328400,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time t=0. To observe those points, I just wait \naround until their light has had time to reach me.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2336000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At every future tick of my clock, a signal arrives \nfrom the left and the right, and I use that to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2340800,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "build up a set of simultaneous events, defining \nmy tals 0 xaxis. Our traveler does the same thing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2349440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but from my point of view, their clock is slow, \nso I see them register signals at a different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2355040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rate. At the same time, they're moving away from \nthe signals coming from the left and towards the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2360560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ones originating on the right, affecting \nwhich signals are seen at a given instant.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2366080,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The traveler infers a set of simultaneous events \nthat to me are not simultaneous, but there is no  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2374160,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "preferred reference frame. Their sloped xaxis is \nright for them. Even just doing this graphically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2382560,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we see that the traveler's x-axis is rotated by \nthe same angle as their time axis. That comes from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2390080,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "insisting that we all see the same speed of light, \n45° on the spac-time diagram. Moving between these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2397600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reference frames is now a simple matter of \nsquaring up our traveler's axes. In fact, we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2403360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grid up the diagram with a set of lines parallel \nto these new axes and square up everything while  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2409760,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maintaining our intersection points. My world \nline is now speeding off to the left while our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2416640,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traveler is motionless. We just performed \na Lorent transformation but using geometry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2423840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than math. This transformation allows you \nto calculate how properties like distance, time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2429280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "velocity, even mass and energy shift between \nreference frames. But check out what happens if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2435440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I attach pens to all of the intersections. When \nI transform between frames, they trace out our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2442480,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hyperbole. Those intersections represent locations \nof space-time events relative to the origin. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2450000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will always land on the same hyperola, no matter \nthe observer's reference frame. I told you that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2456160,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these contours show where clocks moving from the \norigin reach the same proper time count. But more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2462960,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generally, each represents a single value for the \nspace-time interval. The delta x and delta t of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2470000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event at the end point of a traveler's world \nline might change depending on who is watching  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2475840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the hyperbolic contour that they landed \non the space-time interval will not. This is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2482160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because the space-time interval itself comes \ndirectly from the lorren transformation as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2488640,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only measurement of space-time separation that is \nunchanging or invariant under that transformation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2495920,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we can finally get to why this thing is so \nimportant and what it really represents. It may  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2502720,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seem counterintuitive that an event very close to \nthe origin in both space and time can be separated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2510320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from that origin by the same space-time interval \nas an event that is very distant in both space and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2516880,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time. The hyperbolic shape seems to demand that. \nBut remember, it takes the same amount of proper  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2523760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time to travel from the origin to a nearby near \nfuture event compared to a distant far future  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2530320,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event on the same contour. From the point of view \nof a particle communicating some causal influence,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2538720,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those points are equivalent. The space-time \ninterval tracks this causal proximity. We  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2545760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can think of these lines as contours on a sort of \ncausal geography. The way I define the space-time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2551520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interval, it becomes increasingly negative in the \nforward time direction. So we can represent this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2556800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a valley dropping away from me here at the \norigin. I naturally slide through time by the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2562800,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "steepest path straight down. I can change that \npath by expending energy to change my velocity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2569280,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although doing so realines the contours, \nso I always slide down the steepest path.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2575520,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's no point anywhere downhill that \nI can't reach as long as I can get close  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2580080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to the speed of light. In fact, the \nnearest downhill contour defines the forward  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2586560,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light cone for anyone anywhere on the space-time \ndiagram. But uphill is impossible as long as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2594960,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic speed limit is maintained. Breaking that \nspeed limit and sliding uphill are equivalent.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2601680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To reverse the direction of your changing \nspace-time interval is to reverse the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2606240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direction of causality. To travel backwards in \ntime, the space-time diagram we looked at today  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2612480,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was for a flat or manowski space in which faster \nthan light travel is the only way to flip your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2619120,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spac-time interval. But in the crazy curved space \nwithin a black hole, it gets flipped for you.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2626480,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We'll soon see how this requirement of \na forward causal evolution leads to some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2633040,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incredible predictions when we try to calculate \nthe subevent horizon interval of spaceime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2641520,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today on spacetime, we're going to talk about time \nspace or the strange switching in the roles of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2648480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space and time that occurs in the mathematics when \nwe drop below the event horizon of a black hole.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2654240,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What does this bizarre statement space and time \nswitching roles even mean? Is this space-time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2661840,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dyslexia purely a mathematical quirk or does it \ncorrespond to real tiny wimy weirdness? We've been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2670080,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "working up to this one, so you might want to hit \npause and check out these episodes if you think  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2674880,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you need some more background. Okay, let's get \nstarted. First, we'll think about what the flow  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2681040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of time looks like without black holes or even \nspace-time curvature. When we talked about the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2686240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geometry of causality, we saw that this quantity \nthat we called the space-time interval governs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2692560,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the flow of cause and effect. The only reliable \nordering of events in a relative universe. I'm  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2700240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to show you the math one more time, and then \nwe'll get back to doing all of this graphically.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2705760,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The spac-time interval is defined like this for \nboring old flat or Minkovsky space. Different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2712400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observers may report that two events are separated \nby different distances delta x and by different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2718160,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amounts of time delta t. However, all observers \nrecord the same space-time interval. If one event  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2725520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causes a second event, the space-time interval \nmust be zero or negative. That just means that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2732240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a light speeded causal link may have traveled \nbetween them. You could say that an object at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2738560,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a given space-time instant is caused by whatever \nversion of itself existed an instant earlier. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2745840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world lines of objects have decreasing space-time \nintervals. In fact, forward temporal evolution  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2752240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requires a negative space-time interval. In \nflat spacetime, that negative sign in front  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2757680,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the delta t drives that forward evolution. \nThis makes t the timelike coordinate while x is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2766000,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the space-like coordinate. For causality to be \nmaintained, the timelike coordinate must always  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2772400,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increase. Reversing causality means flipping the \nsign of the space-time interval. In our episode on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2779760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "super luminal time travel, we saw that in flat \nspace, this means traveling faster than light,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2785680,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is of course impossible. But if we introduce \na black hole, we now have a second way to flip the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2792640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sign of the space-time interval. We're going to \nsee how this changes the behavior of time in very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2798320,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strange ways. Add a non-rotating uncharged black \nhole and the space-time interval becomes this.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2806480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This comes from Carl Schwartzshield's \nsolution to the Einstein field equations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2811520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the very first accurate description of a black \nhole. I've left out a few terms. This equation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2816720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assumes no orbital motion, only motion towards \nor away from the center of the black hole, which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2822240,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a distance r away. That RS is the swast shield \nradius, the radius of the event horizon. Very far  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2830480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the event horizon, the swast shield interval \nbecomes the good old Minkovsky interval. and time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2836400,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and space are nicely separated. But if an object \ngets close to the event horizon, so are just a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2843920,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little bit bigger than RS, that stuff in the two \nbrackets describes extreme warping of spaceime.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2851520,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as long as you're outside the event horizon, \ntime behaves itself mostly, a negative space-time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2858800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interval still means causal movement. And the only \nway to break causality is still with faster than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2864640,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light travel. Things change radically below the \nevent horizon. When R gets smaller than RS, then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2871840,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both of these brackets become negative. The entire \ndeltar stuff is now negative and the delta t stuff  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2878800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is positive. Below the event horizon, there is \nonly one way to maintain the respectable causal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2885040,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "progression expected of a well-mannered temporal \nentity. That's to fall inwards to have a nonzero  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2892880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "delta r. As it happens, you don't have a choice. \nSpace itself is falling inwards faster than the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2898800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed of light towards the central singularity. It \ncarries you with it and drives your personal clock  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2904720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forward as it does so. In the mathematics, \nthe coordinate r which once represents a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2909680,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance now grants the negative sign needed to \nmaintain your causal flow. It becomes timelike.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2917680,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's unidirectional. Meanwhile, the coordinate \npreviously known as time t lost its negative sign  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2925360,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and become space-like. So, it can be traversed in \nany direction or not traversed at all. But what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2932800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "does all of this time space switching actually \nlook like? Let's fall into the black hole one more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2938960,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time now graphically instead of mathematically. \nBack out here in the regular universe, it's pretty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2946320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obvious where the past and the future are. On \nour ever popular space-time diagram, we see a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2952560,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sharp division between the two. Our past light \ncone encompasses all of spaceime that could have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2959200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "influenced us. While our future light cone shows \nus the parts of the universe that we might ever  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2964320,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hope to encounter or influence. Which direction \nis the future? ahead along our time axis and at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2973040,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right angles to all of our space axes. Our future \nlight cone stares fixedly forwards, encompassing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2979840,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all spatial directions equally. This is no longer \ntrue if we introduce gravity. Close to a massive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2986800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object, your future is no longer at right angles \nto space. It becomes slightly tilted in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2992080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direction of that mass. send out a burst of future \ndefining light rays and they won't spread out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2997920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evenly because they bend towards the gravitational \nfield. As you approach the event horizon of a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3003280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole, more and more light rays are turned \ntowards the event horizon. Your future light cone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3009280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and your time axis begin to blur together with \nthe inward radial axis of the black hole. At this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3016160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point, it's time we switch diagrams. Close to and \nwithin the black hole. The Penrose diagram is much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3022640,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more useful. It deals with the extreme stretching \nof space and time by compactifying lines of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3028640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant space or time close to its boundaries. \nWe talked about these diagrams previously,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3033920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but an important thing to remember is that the \nlines of constant space and time are curved so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3039840,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that light cones remain upright and light always \ntravels at a 45° angle even inside the black hole.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3047600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This entire diagonal line represents the event \nhorizon. Watch what happens to our view of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3053440,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe as we approach it. Our entire future \nlight cone encompasses more and more of the event  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3060640,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon. That last tiny sliver is a narrowing \nwindow directly above that you could escape to at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3068480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "close to the speed of light. Meanwhile, our past \nlight cone now encompasses light that has been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3074240,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "struggling to escape from just above the event \nhorizon since the distant past. But we still see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3081360,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nothing from below the horizon. Yet, as soon as we \npass the horizon, everything changes. The outside  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3088880,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe exits our future light cone, which now \njust contains the singularity. We also begin to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3095760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "encounter a new set of photons from the past. At \nthe moment of crossing, light rays from the event  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3100960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon itself are suddenly visible. In fact, we \nplummet through a sea of light that is eternally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3107440,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "climbing outwards but getting nowhere. After that, \nwe have access to the history of the interior of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3113920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole. As we fall with the faster than \nlight flow of spaceime, we overtake light that is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3120160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outward pointing. That light isn't actually making \nheadway outwards. It's trying to swim upstream and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3126480,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "failing against the faster than light cascade of \nspaceime. Some of this light might be from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3133200,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapsing surface of the star that first formed \nthe black hole, emitted long before we entered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3138480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event horizon. It appears to come from below \nus because it's trying to climb upwards. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3144720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "though, it was emitted at larger radi than \nwherever we encounter it. Also, in our past light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3150640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cone are light rays that are pointed inwards. \nsome of them coming from the outside universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3156000,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This light overtakes us as we fall. This is light \nthat entered the event horizon after we did and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3162320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appears to reach us from above. We can try to move \ntowards either source of light, down towards light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3168240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the black holes past or up towards light \nfrom the black hole's future. Those directions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3173600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those spatial freedoms are now described by what \nwas once the time coordinate, but it's no longer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3180080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "timelike. You can traverse it in either direction, \nmaking it space-like. Doing so isn't actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3186560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traveling in time, even though there's a sense of \npast events in one direction, the collapsing star,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3192160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and future events in the other, everything that \nfell into the black hole after us. But remember  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3197120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that our future light cone actually just points \ntowards the singularity. If we try to accelerate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3203120,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in either direction, up or down, we just quicken \nour demise. Best just to fall. It's the last  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3210400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mercy granted by the black hole. It transports \nus to our doom by the slowest path unless we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3216880,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resist. Below the event horizon, there's still \na sense of spatial upness and downness. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3223200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the old radial dimension isn't space-like. It's \ntimelike. Every photon that reaches us was emitted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3229600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at some larger radius than wherever we encounter \nit. Even if it's old light struggling outwards,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3236080,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the past is radially outwards. and all possible \nfuture directions lead radially inwards in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3242880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same way that all world lines move towards \nthe future in the outside universe. Time is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3249120,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "layered radially and r is timelike unidirectional. \nThe singularity becomes a future time not a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3257040,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "central place. In fact, the swast shield metric \nreally gives two separate space-time maps in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3264560,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "single equation. One for above and one for below \nthe event horizon. The coordinates R and T play  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3271200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different roles in those regions. There are \nother coordinate systems in which that switch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3275680,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never happens. But this mysterious dimensional \nflip does give us some fascinating insight into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3282560,
    "dDurationMs": 9120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how time and space blend together in what is \nperhaps the strangest place in all of spaceime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3291680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lurking in the depths of the mathematics of \nEinstein's general relativity is an object even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3297280,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stranger than the mysterious black hole. In fact, \nit's the black holes mirror twin, the white hole.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3304720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some even think that these could be the origin \nof our universe. The astrophysical phenomenon of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3310160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole has captured the imagination \nof scientists and science enthusiasts alike  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3315520,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for many decades. When the idea first emerged \nfrom Einstein's general theory of relativity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3322640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicists wondered how seriously to take this \nmathematical description of an inescapable region  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3328960,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of spaceime. Astronomers have since demonstrated \nthat black holes are very real with convincing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3336080,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence that quazars, x-ray binaries, even the \ncenter of our own Milky Way galaxy harbor these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3342800,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational monstrosities. But the mathematics \nthat predicts the existence of the black hole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3350000,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also describes entities that are even stranger but \nwhose relationship with reality is still unclear.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3358160,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One such entity is the white hole. A white hole \nis the opposite of a black hole in a very literal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3366320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematical sense. In fact, it's a time reversed \nblack hole. A black hole is defined as a region of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3372960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inward flowing spaceime with a one-way boundary \ncalled the event horizon from inside of which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3379760,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nothing can ever escape. That makes a white hole a \nregion of outward flowing spaceime. It also has an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3386880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon, but that horizon prohibits entry, \nnot exit. Nothing outside a white hole can ever  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3393360,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enter, and everything inside must be ejected. Not \neven light can leave a black hole, hence the whole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3400560,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black thing. But light can only leave a white \nhole. So, these might be expected to radiate like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3407680,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crazy, and white would be an understatement. Now, \nbefore everyone gets too excited, white holes are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3415600,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably a figment of mathematical imagination, \nbut they're a fascinating one, and the idea may  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3422640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "help us understand the origin of the universe. \nWhite holes first emerged in the very earliest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3428960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematical description of black holes. Only a \nfew months after Einstein published his general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3434320,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory of relativity, Carl Schwartzshield solved \nits equations for a very particular case. a single  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3441440,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point of mass in an otherwise empty spaceime. \nThe resulting SWAT shield metric actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3447920,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "describes a black hole, the simplest black hole \npossible, one without spin, without charge,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3455200,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or without change. An eternal black hole that \ndoesn't grow or shrink and has always existed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3463920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've talked quite a bit about the bizarre \nbehavior of space and especially time at and below  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3470000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event horizon of a black hole. Here's a little \nplaylist if you want a refresher. But here's the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3475680,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lowdown. The time that happens inside a black \nhole is not part of the past or future history  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3482960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the outside universe. From the perspective of \nan outside observer, any events occurring at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3488400,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon, including falling into it, happen \ninfinitely far in the future. Once you fall into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3494960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole, the swath shield metric tells \nus that space and time switch their roles. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3501440,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "singularity no longer occupies a central location. \nIt now occupies an inevitable future time. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3510000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a real black hole forms from the gravitational \ncollapse of a massive stars core. After the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3516000,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse, the future singularity comes into being. \nAnd in the past, well, there's just a star. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3524080,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what does this idealized eternal black hole look \nlike in the past? If we follow the Swatshield  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3530640,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metric back in time, we find something very \nstrange. We find the singularity again lurking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3538480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinitely far in the past. From the point of view \nof the outside universe, the eternal black hole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3544240,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "singularity exists both in the infinite future \nand in the infinite past. That may sound strange,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3552640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it gets stranger. To really understand what \nthis eternal black hole looks like, we're going to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3558320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to use a tool that we've already played with \nthe Pinrose diagram. To refresh your memory, in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3564320,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose diagram, the X and Y axes are redefined \nfrom space and time to merge space and time into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3571920,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new coordinates. They compactify spaceime so \nthat time bunches up towards the edges and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3579920,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "borders correspond to infinite past and future. \nAlso, lines of constant distance and time curve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3588000,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so that light paths always travel on 45° paths. We \nare hanging out here and now at the center of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3596080,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diagram. If we place an eternal black hole far to \nthe left, then the future left boundary represents  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3603840,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black holes event horizon. Any movement to the \nleft brings you closer to that event horizon. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3610640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon itself is a 45° line. In our weird \nPenrose coordinates, this represents a constant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3616880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance from the center of the black hole. Light \ntraveling at that 45 degree angle takes infinite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3623280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time to escape the event horizon. And the region \nbeyond that line represents the interior of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3629600,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole. There the dimensions of space and time \nswitch roles. The once vertical contours of space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3637040,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are now timelike and flow inexorably towards \nthe future singularity. These two regions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3645600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe and the black hole interior, are \njust the swast shield metric mapped out using  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3651440,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose coordinates. But our map isn't complete. \nRemember, this is an eternal black hole. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3659200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it must exist in the past. Map into the \npast and we see a time reflected version  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3665680,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our future black hole. Everything about it is \ntime reversed. The singularity is a past event.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3672480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space within is timelike. But instead of \nflowing towards the singularity, it flows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3678160,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away. And the event horizon is now a barrier \nto entry, not to exit. We can make some sense  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3685760,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the behavior of this strange region by using \nthe Penrose diagram. Imagine that something in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3692560,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our past was traveling at the speed of light and \ntrying to reach the past event horizon. There's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3698800,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no way it can get there unless it goes faster \nthan light. Oh, it'll reach an event horizon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3706080,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but only the event horizon of our future where it \nplunges into a regular old black hole. Remember  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3713680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that all of this is from our perspective, far from \nthe event horizon. We can never see anything cross  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3719040,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the horizon. The light rays from any crossing \nreach us infinitely far in the future. Even if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3726000,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole plunge began far in the past. So \nthe past region of the eternal black hole has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3734000,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an event horizon that's a barrier to entry. But \nalso light rays within that region must move up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3741600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the diagram that suggests they must exit into \nthe outside universe. Anything inside the past  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3748320,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eternal black hole must be ejected. So far, \nthis region fits perfectly the description of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3755120,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a white hole. The eternal black hole of the \npast technically is a white hole. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3762480,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's not one that we can ever observe for two \nreasons. One, light rays exiting that past white  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3769440,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole can never reach us. The past singularity and \npast event horizon are infinitely far in the past  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3777040,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from our point of view. light has to traverse \ninfinite time to reach our location. And two,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3783840,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there's no such thing as an eternal black hole. \nThe universe hasn't existed for eternity. And it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3790720,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "didn't even begin with black holes in place. Even \nthough this type of white hole isn't observable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3797680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some physicists have taken the description very \nseriously. The math describing the white hole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3803360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a perfectly good use of the swat geometric. \nIt obeys general relativity. It really is just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3809760,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a black hole but viewed backwards in time. Yet \ngeneral relativity is time reversal symmetric.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3816480,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Something that can happen forwards in time should \nalso be able to happen in reverse. So can new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3823280,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "white holes actually form? Well, theoretically \nyes. But to make one, you need to reverse entropy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3832240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "See, although it's possible to build a white \nhole in general relativity, there are other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3837040,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "laws of physics that the universe needs to obey. \nFor example, the second law of thermodynamics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3844000,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It demands that entropy, a measure of disorder, \nalways increase. This law defines the direction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3851440,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the flow of time. To reverse time, you need \nto break the law. You need to decrease entropy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3858320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, this is technically possible because entropy \nis a statistical phenomenon. Very rare reductions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3864400,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in entropy do happen. As long as globally entropy \nincreases on average, it's conceivable that an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3872480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incredibly rare entropy dip could lead to an \neffective reversal of time and a white hole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3877760,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could form. However, it would immediately explode \nin a burst of energy as soon as entropy and time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3884960,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resumed their normal flow upwards and forwards. We \nactually did talk about a case where a random drop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3892240,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in entropy led to something very much like a white \nhole in this episode. It's been speculated that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3899360,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Big Bang itself came from such a profoundly \nimprobable entropy dip. And as it happens,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3907040,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Big Bang looks mathematically at least much \nlike a white hole. It's an expanding outpouring of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3915200,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spaceime containing a vast amount of energy. And \nthe bang itself can never be entered. After all,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3922400,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's in the past. The difference between the Big \nBang and a white hole is that the former possesses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3928560,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no singularity. It happened everywhere at the same \ntime. Still, that hasn't stopped physicists from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3935440,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "having fun with the idea. It's been proposed that \nwhen a black hole forms, a white hole forms on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3942160,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the opposite side. Energy entering the black hole \nexits the white hole. Physicist Lee Smolen takes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3950960,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it a step further to suggest that the resulting \nwhite hole is the big bang of a new baby universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3958960,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that in fact our universe formed that way. \nMore on that another time. But speaking of other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3966640,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universes, it turns out that we haven't finished \nbuilding our Penrose diagram yet. The past white  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3973600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole was revealed when we traced the eternal \nblack hole backwards in time. In fact, what we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3979200,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "did was to maximally extend spaceime. We required \nthat all paths be traceable through infinite past  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3986720,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and future space, provided they don't hit the \nsingularity. But what about light rays entering or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3993600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leaving our eternal black hole from the opposite \nside? The mathematics of the swast shield metric  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3999360,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "describes an entirely independent region of \nspaceime parallel to our own. It looks like an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4007520,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "identical alternate universe on the other side of \nthe black hole accessible through what we call an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4014400,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein Rosen bridge, better known as a wormhole. \nIn the not too distant future, we'll investigate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4022080,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the reality of this mysterious parallel patch of \nspaceime. He was perhaps the greatest genius of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4030960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our time. Steven Hawking peered behind the curtain \nof reality and glimpsed the true workings of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4037280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe. He inspired all of us to pursue our \ncuriosity no matter the obstacles. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4043680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his true legacy is his work. He made profound \ncontributions across physics from quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4048960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory to cosmology. Our tribute is to bring you \nSteven Hawkings most famous discovery. I'm Matt  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4055440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Odow. This is spaceime and it's time for Hawking \nradiation. Soon after Einstein revealed his great  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4062480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "general theory of relativity in 1915, physicists \nrealized that it allowed for the possibility of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4068880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "catastrophic gravitational collapse in places of \nextreme density like the dead core of a massive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4074240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star. Space and time could be dragged inwards \nto create a hole in the universe, a boundary  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4080960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in spaceime called an event horizon that could \nbe entered but from beyond which nothing could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4087120,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "return. Once formed, there was nothing in theory \nor imagination that could bring material consumed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4093840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back to the outside universe. These black \nholes should exist forever, only growing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4100160,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never shrinking. Or so we thought, until 1974 \nwhen a young physicist named Steven Hawking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4106720,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "published a paper in Nature entitled Black Hole \nExplosions. In this and in a follow-up 1975 paper,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4114480,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he attempted a new union of quantum mechanics and \ngeneral relativity to show that black holes should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4120480,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not be so black after all. They should leak. They \nshould emit what we now know as Hawking radiation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4127520,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's a popular description of how Hawking \nradiation works. It goes something like this.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4131920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Empty space seas with activity as pairs of virtual \nparticles matter and antimatter spontaneously  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4138320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appear and then annihilate each other briefly \nborrowing energy from the vacuum itself. But when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4144160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this happens near a black hole, sometimes one of \nthe pair will be swallowed by the event horizon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4150000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leaving the other free to escape and taking \nits stolen energy with it. That energy can't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4155280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "come from nothing, and so the black hole itself \npays the debt by slowly leaking away its mass.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4161440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a nice picture, but how accurate is it? \nIn fact, if we follow the narrative of Hawkings  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4166960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "original calculation, the story sounds rather \ndifferent. We've come a long way over the past  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4171920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "few months, building up the knowledge we'll need \nto follow that calculation. Re-watching some of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4177120,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those episodes either now or after this video will \nbe helpful. But if you think you're ready, let's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4182080,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take a deep dive into the quantum field theory \nof curved spaceime to glimpse the true nature of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4188480,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking radiation. Actually, a quick QFT refresher \ncan't hurt. Space is filled with quantum fields.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4195920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They can oscillate with different frequencies, \nmuch like the many possible vibrational modes on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4200240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a guitar string. A particle is like a note on \nthe string. And just like a real guitar note,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4206000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real particles tend to be comprised of many \nvibrational modes. Those underlying vibrational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4211200,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modes are still present in the absence of real \nparticles. They fluctuate in energy due to quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4216480,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertainty. And those fluctuations give us what \nwe think of as virtual particles. Now, don't take  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4222640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the existence of virtual particles too seriously. \nThey're really just a tool for calculating the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4228320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite ways in which a fluctuating quantum \nfield can behave. One way that quantum fields  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4233920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are very different to guitar strings is that they \ncan have both positive and negative frequencies.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4239760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A negative frequency can be thought of as \na mode that travels backwards in time and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4244240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be interpreted as corresponding to antimatter. \nNow, that's a whole level of weird all on its own.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4250000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we talk about it here. When a quantum field \nis in a vacuum state, there's a balance between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4255680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "positive and negative frequency modes, which \nyou can crudely think of as a balance between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4260720,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "virtual matter and antimatter particles. These all \nvirtually annihilate or cancel out so that no real  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4268160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles exist. This is all fine in flat space, \nbut spatial curvature can mess with the balance of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4274560,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the underlying quantum field modes by introducing \nhorizons. Horizons cut off access to certain modes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4281520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the quantum fields, disturbing the balance that \ndefines the vacuum. Steven Hawking knew that black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4287440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes with their insane space-time curvature would \nwreak havoc on quantum fields in their vicinity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4293520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what would the effect be? To answer that \nproperly, he would need a full union of general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4299120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity and quantum mechanics, a theory of \nquantum gravity, a theory of everything. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4305600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "didn't exist then and it doesn't exist yet. Not \nto be deterred by the impossible, Hawing came up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4312320,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with an ingenious workaround. The narrative of \nHawings mathematics goes something like this.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4319040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He imagined a single space-time path, a light \nspeeded trajectory called a null geodisic. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4325280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extends from far in the past to far in the \nfuture. This is a perilous path. It passes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4330960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the location of a black hole in \nthe instant before it forms. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4335280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it is the very last trajectory to do so. It \nemerges barely ahead of the forming event  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4340400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon. Hawking imagined a simple quantum field \ntracing this path. a field that is in a perfect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4346400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vacuum state before the formation of the black \nhole. But he found that the close shave with the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4351600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole disturbs the fundamental vibrational \nmodes that define the fluctuations of the vacuum.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4357520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the time this trajectory has found its way back \nout into flat space again, those fluctuations look  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4363040,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like real particles. A distant future observer \nsees radiation coming from the black hole.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4369600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawkings imaginary path from the distant past to \nthe distant future was brilliant. It allowed him  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4375280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to compare the state of the vacuum in two regions \nof flat space far from the black hole. Regions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4381040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where the nature of vacuums, quantum fields, and \nparticles are perfectly well understood. But to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4386880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understand the effect of the close encounter with \nthe black hole, he required an uneasy marriage of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4392080,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum mechanics and general relativity. In \nthe absence of a theory of quantum gravity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4396880,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking needed a hack. That hack was the Boliv \ntransformations. Say that three times fast.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4404160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These can be used to approximate the effect of \ncurved spacetime on quantum fields by smoothly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4409200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "connecting regions of flat space. They describe \na sort of mixing of the positive and negative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4414320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frequency vibrational modes that are caused by \nthat curved space. The physical interpretation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4419840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this mixing via the Bolivia transformations \nis tricky. In fact, there isn't just one valid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4425520,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interpretation. Hawings calculation talks about \nscattering. Certain modes of the quantum field are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4431680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scattered or deflected by the gravitational field \nof the forming black hole. They are nudged off  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4436800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their narrow escape path and so are lost behind \nthe forming event horizon. Meanwhile, other modes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4442560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "avoid scattering and continue unscathed. With \nthe loss of certain fundamental modes, the vacuum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4447520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state must be constructed from the remaining \nmodes. That distorted vacuum looks like it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4453280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "full of particles. The nature of the lost modes \ntells us what Hawking radiation should look like.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4459280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black holes tend to scatter modes with wavelengths \nsimilar to their own sizes. The quantum field that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4464720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emerges is distorted in the same wavelength \nrange. And so it produces wave packets. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4469840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produces particles that also have wavelengths \nabout as large as the event horizon. So the more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4475520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive the black hole, the longer the wavelength \nof its radiation. Hawking calculated the frequency  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4481200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distribution of this radiation and found something \nincredible. It should look exactly like thermal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4486880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation. Black holes should have a heat glow \nwith an apparent temperature that depends on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4492320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their mass. More directly, it's proportional to \nthe surface area of the event horizon. Large black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4498400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes should appear cold, radiating excruciatingly \nslowly, but small black holes should appear hot  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4504320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the smallest should radiate explosively. \nOkay, so what about the whole picture of particle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4509920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "antiparticle pairs being pulled apart by the event \nhorizon? So, Hawings math describes splitting or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4516160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mixing of these pure positive and negative \nfrequency modes. It's fair to interpret this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4521680,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mixing as the promotion of what were once virtual \nparticles into reality. And for the escaping  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4528320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modes, there exist a corresponding set of modes \nlinked by quantum entanglement that are trapped  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4533920,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behind the event horizon. We can interpret those \nas corresponding to the swallowed antiparticle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4539440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "partner. So the split matter antimatter part of \nthe picture is reasonable. But there are reasons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4545680,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to dismiss aspects of this picture. Firstly, this \nradiation is not localized. Remember the Hawking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4552640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation has wavelengths the size of the event \nhorizon, the size of the entire black hole. Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4557840,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these are the deu wavelengths of created \nparticles and they tell us that there is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4562480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an enormous quantum uncertainty in the location \nof these particles. Hawking radiation must appear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4568240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to come from the global black hole, not from \nspecific points on the event horizon. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4573680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an observer in freef fall through the horizon \nsees nothing. To them, space is locally flat. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4579920,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vacuum should look like a vacuum. This radiation \nis visible only to distant observers. Well, there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4587200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is one exception. When you turn on your jetpack \nand hover a fixed distance above the horizon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4592720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then you do see particles. you see unrew \nradiation. We'll look at its relationship  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4597840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to Hawking radiation in the future. By the \nway, Hawking radiation is mostly going to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4603120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons and other massless particles. To produce \nparticles with mass, the energy of the radiation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4608400,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has to be high enough to cover the rest mass \nof the particle. So, it's okay to interpret the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4614480,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "narrative of Hawkings calculation as the splitting \nof entangled matter and antimatter pairs. Even if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4619440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it really is just a huristic interpretation, it's \nthe cause of the splitting that's hard to pin  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4624800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down. We can think about positive and negative \nfrequency modes being mixed due to scattering,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4630080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps by the as yet undiscovered graviton. \nOther physicists have derived Hawkings result  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4635520,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with very different seeming narratives. For \nexample, in 2001, Periq and Wilchek got the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4641680,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same thermal spectrum for Hawking radiation by \nthinking about particles escaping from beneath  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4646560,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event horizon through quantum tunneling. The \ncommon thread is quantum uncertainty. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4653760,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertainty in positional momentum can lead \nto particle pairs that were once in the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4658080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "location or modes that were once on the same world \nline becoming separated by the event horizon.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4664240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alternatively, uncertainty in energy can lead to \nparticle creation. Whichever way you interpret it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4670320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's hard to avoid the conclusion that \nblack holes emit particles. The fact  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4674560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that different derivations lead to exactly the \nsame result or that the radiation looks thermal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4679600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can't be by chance. It's hard to make Hawking \nradiation go away in the math. And believe me,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4685600,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Steven Hawking himself tried. Ultimately, however, \nthese calculations are all hacks, albeit utterly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4693280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brilliant ones. Without a full quantum theory \nof gravity, the origin of Hawking radiation will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4698960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remain mysterious. And there are other mysteries \nthat we haven't touched on. For example, what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4705040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happens to the particles or modes trapped by the \nblack hole? How do they end up reducing the black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4710240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole's mass instead of increasing it? And then \nthere's the famous information paradox in which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4715680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking radiation appears to destroy what should \nbe a conserved quantity, quantum information.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4722080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We'll tackle all of these in future episodes. For \nnow, we must conclude that black holes radiate and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4728160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in doing so evaporate. The scariest monsters of \ngeneral relativity are ultimately unraveled by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4734560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the brilliant mind of Steven Hawking and \na mysterious quirk of quantum spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4743280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black holes seem like they should have no entropy, \nbut in fact, they hold most of the entropy in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4749120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe. Let's figure this out. At first, it \nseemed that black holes were so simple they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4753760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should have no entropy. Well, it turns out they \ncontain most of the entropy in the universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4759520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's see why. Because this fact may force us \nto conclude that the universe is a hologram.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4765520,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black holes are a problem. They are the inevitable \nresult of extreme gravitational collapse. At least  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4771920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they are inevitable according to the equations \nof Einstein's general theory of relativity. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4777600,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory is one of the most thoroughly tested in \nall of physics, which means we should probably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4782000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "believe in black holes. Also, we've seen them in \ntheir gravitational effects on their surrounding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4787680,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space and in the gravitational waves caused \nwhen they merge. And yet, if black holes exist,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4794320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which apparently they do, they contradict other \ntheories in physics that are as sacred as general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4799440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity. They cause all sorts of problems with \nquantum theory, which we've talked about before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4804320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we'll review in a sec. But they also present \nan apparent conflict with the notion of entropy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4810160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the second law of thermodynamics. It was while \npondering that conflict that Jacob Beckenstein  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4816080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "realized an incredible connection between black \nholes and thermodynamics. His insight launched an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4822240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entire new way of thinking about the universe \nin terms of information theory and ultimately  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4827600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "led to the holographic principle, which I promise \nwe're getting to and are almost there. But first,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4833440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are going to need to know more about why \nblack holes contain most of the universe's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4838400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entropy. Okay, I'm getting way ahead of myself. \nLet's actually rewind back to those episodes where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4844000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we laid out the black hole information paradox \nbecause they're going to be critical to a proper  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4848560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understanding. We're also rewinding to the late \n'60s, early '7s when physicists realized something  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4854080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "odd about black holes. What they realized is that \nit doesn't matter what material goes into one.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4859600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the point of view of the outside universe, \nblack holes can only have three properties: mass,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4865040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spin, and electric charge. This is the so-called \nno hair theorem. And it suggests that most of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4870640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the information about anything that falls into a \nblack hole is lost to the outside universe. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4876320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a fundamental tenant of quantum mechanics is that \nquantum information can never be destroyed. So if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4881840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes evaporate, as Hawking discovered and \nwe also covered, this evaporation should destroy a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4887680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole's internal quantum information, giving \nus the black hole information paradox. Eventually,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4893680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a possible resolution to this paradox was \nfound by Gerard. He described a mechanism  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4899440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by which the information contained by infalling \nparticles could be preserved on the event horizon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4904800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the black hole. From there, it could be \nimprinted on the outgoing Hawking radiation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4910160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allowing the information to escape back into \nthe universe. Okay, problem solved. But in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4915600,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our previous episodes, we skipped the key insight \nthat started all of this. It all began with Jacob  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4922160,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Beckinstein thinking about black hole entropy. \nOkay, first entropy. Yeah, we talked about that a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4929200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lot recently. Also, you know, it's almost like all \nof those episodes are starting to come together.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4934960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Almost like we planned this. Go and watch that \nbackground stuff if you're behind. But of course,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4940640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for now, I'll give you a quick TLDW on entropy. \nSo, we can think of entropy in two ways. One,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4946880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's a measure of how evenly energy is spread \nout. High entropy means thermal equilibrium.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4952480,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So energy is very evenly distributed and can't be \nextracted in a useful way. And two, entry measures  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4959120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the amount of unknown information that you would \nneed to perfectly describe the systems internal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4963600,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state like all the particle positions, \nvelocities, etc. The higher the entropy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4967600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the more randomly distributed its particles and \nthe more possible configurations lead to the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4972720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "macroscopic state. The higher the entropy, \nthe less you can guess about the properties  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4977360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of individual particles based on the global \nproperties like temperature, volume, pressure,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4982320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc. Okay, so the second law of thermodynamics \nstates that entropy of an isolated system must  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4988720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always increase which means energy tends to \nspread out evenly and particles tend to randomize  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4994080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reducing our information about their microscopic \nstates. How does this relate to black holes? Let's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5000400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make a black hole and see what happens to entropy. \nWe start as usual by collapsing the core of a dead  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5006720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star. Now that's a high entropy beast, super \nhot and full of randomly moving particles. We  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5012400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have almost no information about the individual \nparticles, but that information still exists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5017360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the universe, like I guess the particles know \nwhere they are. At the instant the star collapses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5022000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far enough to form an event horizon, it becomes \na black hole. We go from knowing next to nothing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5027440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the object to knowing everything. \nWe can easily measure its mass, spin,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5032400,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and electric charge. And according to the no hair \ntheorem, that's all there is to know. The region  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5038480,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of space in which the black hole formed appears to \nhave gone from high entropy to zero entropy in an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5044640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "instant, shattering the second law in the process, \nwhich to put it mildly is a problem. But if you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5050320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paid attention to the whole information paradox \nbit, you might be able to think of a solution.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5055360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If quantum information is stored on the surface of \nthe black hole, can't we store entropy there also?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5061360,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then why not radiate the entropy back into the \nuniverse as Hawking radiation? Actually, yeah. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5068160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resolution to the information paradox also saves \nthe second law of thermodynamics. That was easy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5073840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I thought physics was supposed to be hard. Okay, \nhang on. Let's think about this a little bit more.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5080000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was this seeming violation of the second law \nthat got Jacob Beckinstein thinking about the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5085280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "connection between black holes and information \nin the first place. The breakthrough insight was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5090800,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this simple observation. The surface area of \na black hole event horizon can never decrease  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5097760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least not according to general relativity. \nSo you know how nothing can escape black holes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5102560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ignoring corking radiation for the moment. That \nshould mean that black holes can only grow. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5108000,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can never shrink in mass or radius. Well, that's \nnot quite true. If you merge two black holes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5115040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some of their mass gets converted to the energy \nradiated away in gravitational waves. There's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5120480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also the Penrose process in which you can extract \nrotational energy of a spinning black hole. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5126240,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by you, I mean not you, I mean super advanced far \nfuture civilizations. Gravitational radiation and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5133120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Penrose process reduce black hole mass \nand radius or the sum of masses and radio  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5138720,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "merging black holes. But there's one property of \nblack holes that no process other than Hawking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5142800,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation can decrease. That's the surface area \nof the event horizon. Do anything to black holes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5149200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their total surface area can only grow or stay \nconstant. Beckenstein saw a close correspondence  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5155040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between the always increasing event horizon \nsurface area and the always increasing nature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5159760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of entropy. He also realized that the equation \nrelating the change in black hole surface area  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5165680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the change in its mass closely resembles the \noriginal definition of thermodynamic entropy. Just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5172240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "replace change in entropy and internal thermal \nenergy with change in black hole surface area and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5177760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole mass respectively. You can also add the \nwork done when you extract energy from the black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5183200,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole and it looks the same as the equation \nfor the work extracted from a thermodynamic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5188480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system. Beckinstein had just discovered black \nhole thermodynamics, but that didn't give him  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5194080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the exact definition for black hole entropy. For \nthat, he turned to Ludvig Boltzman'sformational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5200320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "definition for entropy. So entropy can be \ndefined as the information hidden in a systems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5206240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "microscopic configuration times the Boltzman \nconstant. Beckenstein estimated the amount of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5212320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information that would be lost into a black hole \nas it grew. Essentially, he built a black hole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5217920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out of idealized elementary particles that each \ncontained a single bit of information. And guess  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5224320,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what? The information content of a black hole is \nproportional not to its mass or radius or volume,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5230800,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is proportional to its surface area. In fact, the \ninformation content is very close to that surface  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5237840,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "area divided by the number of plunk areas. It's \nas though each of these minimum possible quant of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5244800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "area each contain a single bit of information. \nNow just multiply that information content by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5251040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Boltzman constant and you have the entropy \nof a black hole which is going to be directly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5255920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proportional to the surface area of the event \nhorizon. Beckenstein's connection between surface  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5260720,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "area and entropy could have been a coincidence. \nAt least until Steven Hawking came along. In 1974,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5268480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a year after Beckenstein's first paper on black \nhole thermodynamics, Hawking published his first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5273520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking radiation paper. He showed that black \nholes radiate random particles exactly as though  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5278800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they have a heat glow of a particular temperature \nthat depends on their mass. So if black holes have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5285360,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a temperature, then they also have entropy. Good \nold-fashioned thermodynamic entropy tells us that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5292240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "change in entropy is change in internal thermal \nenergy divided by temperature. So Hawking just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5297760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plugged his Hawking temperature into that equation \nalong with black hole mass for internal energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5303280,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and figured out the total entropy contained in a \nblack hole. He got an expression almost identical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5309520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to Beckenstein's but just a slightly different \nconstant of proportionality. So you get the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5314720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "result for black hole entropy whether you figure \nit out from the amount of information that gets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5318960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trapped building a black hole or the amount \nof heat that leaks as it evaporates. And it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5325280,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proportional to the surface area. How bizarrely \nconsistent. I'd say that means it's right.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5332240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second law of thermodynamics is saved \nbecause black holes do have entropy. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5337360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they have enormous entropies, the maximum \npossible. So much that black holes are now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5343440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "believed to contain most of the entropy in the \nuniverse. But the real importance of this work  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5349360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wasn't the solution to some obscure conundrum. It \nchanged our thinking about the informationational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5355040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "content of the universe. Beckinstein's formula \nwas derived for black holes, but it also gives  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5360800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the maximum amount of information that can be \nfit into any volume of space. In this respect,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5366400,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's called the Beckenstein bound and it's \nproportional to the surface area of that space.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5372480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is unexpected. Surely, the maximum amount of \ninformation you can fit into some patch of space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5378720,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depends on the volume of that space, as in one bit \nper tiny volume element inside that space. But in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5386240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fact, the rule is one bit per tiny area element \non the surface of that space. That also means  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5392560,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the information needed to describe any volume \nof space, no matter its contents, is proportional  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5398800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the area bounding that space. I've hinted \nonce or twice that this simple idea led to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5404640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holographic principle. The idea that the entire \n3D volume of the universe is just a projection of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5410320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information encoded on a 2D surface surrounding \nthe universe. You just need to add a little bit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5416160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of string theory. It's a hell of a conceptual leap \ngiven it started with Jacob Beckenstein noticing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5422000,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a peculiar similarity between some formula. It \nmight also be true and obviously we'll be back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5429520,
    "dDurationMs": 9280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before too long to talk about string theory and \nthe holographic nature of spaceime. These black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5438800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stones are volcanic rock and this is one of the \nyoungest patches of land on planet Earth. But that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5444400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same geological event that built this land has \nprovided another window. It allows us to observe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5450400,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a time when the universe was still cooling from \nthe fire of its own formation. And to see this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5456560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all we have to do is travel to a telescope on \ntop of the tallest volcano in the world. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5462400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we're driving up to the summit of Monaco on the \nbig island of Hawaii. This is the tallest volcano  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5467600,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the planet that 4200 m. The oxygen up here \nis 60% sea level, but astronomers deal with it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5475600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it is the premier astronomical observing \nsite in the northern hemisphere. To Hawaiians,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5482080,
    "dDurationMs": 12400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it is a sacred site. And to astronomers, \nit's where the Earth meets the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5494480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wow, it's amazing up here. It's like being on \nanother planet. I can already feel the effect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5500960,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the thinner atmosphere. My natural impulse, \nbizarrely, is to hold my breath. Must remember  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5507680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to keep breathing. Here we have 13 of the greatest \ntelescopes in the world operated by 11 different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5513840,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "countries. We have the Japanese Subaru telescope, \nthe Twin Kemes. Over here we have the Canada,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5520080,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "France, Hawaii telescope, and this is Gemini. \nThat's where we're going. We're here to talk  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5527040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about a very special observation. In the \nspring of 2017, astronomers turned Gemini's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5533120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "great mirror towards the constellation of \nBuotis, the plowman. They were looking for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5538960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a faint speck of light that had been noticed in \none of our great surveys of the sky. Astronomers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5544960,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guessed the speck was a quazar, a vortex of \nradiant matter falling into a giant black hole.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5553360,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now quazars are the most luminous objects \nin the universe. What was strange about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5557520,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this one was its distance. Its light was so \nred that astronomers realized that that light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5564560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "must have been stretched out, redshifted by \ntraveling many billions of years through our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5570080,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanding universe. The quazar appeared to be \nmore distant than any we had ever seen. But that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5576640,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn't mean we can't unravel their mysteries. \nAnd Gemini did exactly that. To find out how,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5584240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we're going to need to go inside. \nYou've got to see this. It's incredible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5590880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meet the Gemini telescope. This is what a \nworldass telescope looks like these days. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5597360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is enormous. I still remember the first time \nI came to a telescope like this. It blew me  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5601760,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away. Look at the size of this thing. This is \nour window to the universe. It's cold in here.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5609200,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They keep the dome at the temperature of \nthe upcoming night so that giant structure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5613440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn't warp and twist with the change in \ntemperature. That's a little below freezing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5617440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right now. And you hear that sound? That's the \ncryogenics. They keep the sensitive infrared  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5623280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cameras at 15 above absolute zero. Let's \nactually talk about light for a second.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5629600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Light is a wave and the wavelength of that \nwave determines the properties of light.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5634640,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, visible light, the wavelength \nrange that our eyes are sensitive to,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5638880,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spans only a tiny fraction of the spectrum. That's \nwhy we create telescopes. The universe looks very,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5645200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very different at different wavelengths. For \nexample, viewed in visible light, the Andromeda  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5650400,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy shows us newborn stars. Our atmosphere is \ntransparent to visible light. So, a groundbased  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5656960,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "telescope can see a visible universe as can we. \nGemini is built to be sensitive to the infrared.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5665040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The infrared andrometer is a swirl of star forming \nclouds and gas. Some infrared light also makes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5670720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it through the atmosphere, though it helps to \nbe up here on a mountaintop. Although the air  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5675920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "above the observatory is crystal clear, it still \nblurs distant light somewhat. Turbulence in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5682000,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atmosphere causes incoming wave fronts of light to \nbe warped, and it blurs our view. To correct this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5688960,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gemini uses adaptive optics. It has a deformable \nmirror that flexes and bends to match and correct  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5696720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the warping of incoming light. To do this in real \ntime, Gemini creates its own artificial guide star  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5703040,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by shooting lasers to twinkle off sodium atoms \nat 90 km height, right off the edge of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5713760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the instrument used to analyze the most \ndistant quazar. It's the Gemini North infrared  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5719280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spectrograph. Genius. A spectrograph takes \nincoming light and breaks it into its component  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5724960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavelengths similar to a prism. And it records \nhow much energy is received at each wavelength. We  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5731040,
    "dDurationMs": 9120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call that a spectrum. When the light analyzed by \nthis machine left its quazar, it was ultraviolet.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5740160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But traveling through the expanding universe \nsapped energy and stretched the wavelength of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5745120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that light so that it was infrared by the time \nit reached the earth. And this spectrograph the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5750720,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "red shift tells us how long that light has \nbeen traveling. 13.1 billion years, meaning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5758160,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quazar lived when the universe was only 5% \nits current age. There's a broad blank patch in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5765440,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quazar spectrum. It's a stretch of nothing \nthat tells us a ton. Shortly after the Big Bang,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5772240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when things had cooled down a bit, the universe \nwas filled with hydrogen gas. It was murky,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5778480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially for ultraviolet light. Now, that \ngas collapsed into the very first stars. Then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5784720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the very first galaxies. Those stars eventually \nmelted away the remaining hydrogen in a process  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5790640,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called reionization, leaving a crystal clearar \nuniverse. But this quazar shines out from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5797760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "era of those first stars before they'd finished \nthe job of reionization. Much of the quazar's once  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5804160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ultraviolet light was sucked up before it escaped \nthe early universe. And what about the super  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5810080,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive black hole at the center of the quazar? \nThe same signature wavelengths used to measure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5814800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "red shift are also broadened due to the extreme \nspeeds of matter moving near the black hole.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5820960,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That allows us to estimate the mass of the black \nhole. 800 million suns. If it replaced our sun,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5828560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it would easily swallow Saturn's orbit. Scientists \nstruggle to figure out how it could grow to that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5834160,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "insane size in a tiny fraction of the age of the \nuniverse. We are expanding our understanding of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5841760,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics to figure this one out. That tiny \nspeck is both a revelation and a mystery.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5848640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It literally shines a light on the earliest \nepochs of our universe, teaching us about our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5853760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most fundamental origins. But it also opens \nnew questions. And our great telescopes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5859680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our portals to the universe, past and \npresent, will tackle those questions, too,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5864480,
    "dDurationMs": 10160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and ultimately bring us closer to understanding \nthis mysterious, this magnificent spaceime."
    } ]
  } ]
}
